>> A1: Copy here the declaration of each new or changed `struct' or
>> `struct' member, global or static variable, `typedef', or
>> enumeration.  Identify the purpose of each in 25 words or less.

Planned argument-startup record in userprog/process.c:

struct process_start
  {
    char *command_page;
    struct child_status *status;
  };

command_page: Kernel-owned copy of the command line, limited to one page.
status: Shared record used to report loading and process termination.

The child will also allocate an argc-entry array of char pointers on the
kernel heap. It first holds token pointers, then their user-stack
addresses. The command page, array, and startup record are temporary;
they will be freed before entering user mode or on loading failure.
No argument strings or large arrays will be placed on the kernel stack.

---- ALGORITHMS ----

>> A2: Briefly describe how you implemented argument parsing.  How do
>> you arrange for the elements of argv[] to be in the right order?
>> How do you avoid overflowing the stack page?

The command copy must contain a terminator within 4,096 bytes.
An empty command or a command containing only spaces will fail. A first,
non-destructive scan will count tokens and their string bytes, including
one terminator per token. Repeated spaces will not create empty arguments.

Let N be argc and S the total string bytes. Before allocating the argument
array or writing the stack, the loader will check:

    padding = (4 - S % 4) % 4
    needed  = S + padding + 4 * (N + 1) + 12
    needed <= PGSIZE

This includes the strings, word alignment, N argument pointers, the NULL
sentinel, argv, argc, and a fake return address. It bounds argc by the
actual stack space rather than an arbitrary argument-count limit.

The child will tokenize once with strtok_r(command_page, " ", &save_ptr).
It will copy strings to the user stack in reverse argument order, recording
each resulting user address in the array. After padding, it will push
argv[N] = NULL, the addresses in reverse order, argv, argc, and zero as
the return address. argv[0] will therefore identify the executable name.
Every decrement of the stack pointer will also be checked against the
bottom of the allocated stack page.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> A3: Why does Pintos implement strtok_r() but not strtok()?

strtok() keeps its parsing position in shared static storage. Two
threads parsing commands could overwrite each other's position after a
context switch. strtok_r() instead stores the position through the
caller's save_ptr. Each load can maintain its own parsing state.

>> A4: In Pintos, the kernel separates commands into a executable name
>> and arguments.  In Unix-like systems, the shell does this
>> separation.  Identify at least two advantages of the Unix approach.

A shell can handle quotes, escaping, variable expansion, and wildcards
before starting a program. The kernel then receives the intended argument
boundaries without needing to understand shell syntax.

Different shells can also provide different command languages without
changing the kernel. Programs started without a shell can supply an argv
array directly. Keeping these policies outside the kernel reduces its
parser complexity and the amount of privileged code.

