Planned records in userprog/process.h:

struct child_status
  {
    tid_t pid;
    bool load_ok;
    bool load_reported;
    int exit_status;
    struct semaphore load_done;
    struct semaphore exit_done;
    struct lock ref_lock;
    unsigned refs;
    struct list_elem elem;
  };

pid: Child identifier, set by the child before reporting load completion.
load_ok: Whether ELF loading and argument-stack construction succeeded.
load_reported: Whether the child has already signaled load_done.
exit_status: Result returned by wait; initially -1.
load_done: Signals that the loader has reported its result.
exit_done: Signals that process cleanup and exit-status publication finished.
ref_lock: Protects the shared reference count.
refs: Parent and child ownership references; initially two.
elem: Links the record into its direct parent's child list.

Planned descriptor record in userprog/syscall.c:

struct fd_entry
  {
    int fd;
    struct file *file;
    struct list_elem elem;
  };

fd: Process-local descriptor number, starting at two.
file: Independently opened file handle and current offset.
elem: Links this entry into the process's descriptor list.

Planned USERPROG members of struct thread:

struct list children;
struct child_status *own_status;
struct list open_files;
int64_t next_fd;
struct file *executable;
char *process_name;
int exit_status;
bool is_user_process;

children: Records for direct children that have not been waited on.
own_status: This process's shared child-status record, or NULL.
open_files: Descriptor entries owned by this process.
next_fd: Next descriptor candidate; a wide counter avoids signed overflow.
executable: Open executable handle retained until cleanup.
process_name: Full executable name without arguments, stored outside thread.name.
exit_status: Process result; initialized to -1 for abnormal termination.
is_user_process: Distinguishes user-process termination from kernel-thread exit.

Planned global in userprog/syscall.c:

struct lock filesys_lock;

filesys_lock: Serializes filesystem operations, including executable loading.

The startup record in A1 will pass the command and status to the child.
All thread lists and defaults will be initialized before thread creation
can publish the thread. Shared records and FD nodes will use heap storage.

>> B2: Describe how file descriptors are associated with open files.
>> Are file descriptors unique within the entire OS or just within a
>> single process?

Each process will own a list of fd_entry records. open() will create a
new struct file, allocate an entry, and assign the next available number
from two upward. Descriptors are unique only within their owning process.
Zero and one remain reserved for keyboard input and console output.

Children will begin with empty descriptor lists. Opening the same file
again will create a different handle with an independent offset. close()
will remove the matching entry, close that handle, and free the node.
Process cleanup will close every remaining entry. Failed node allocation
will close the newly opened handle before returning -1. Values beyond
INT_MAX will be rejected before converting next_fd to an int.

---- ALGORITHMS ----

>> B3: Describe your code for reading and writing user data from the
>> kernel.

The syscall dispatcher will copy the call number and its exact number
of 32-bit arguments from the user stack with a checked copy helper. It
will not read f->esp through an unchecked kernel pointer. Unknown calls
and invalid user addresses will terminate the process with status -1.

The dispatcher will handle halt, exit, exec, wait, create, remove, open,
filesize, read, write, seek, tell, and close. Each handler will use kernel
copies of strings and return its result through the saved eax register.

For a nonempty span starting at address U, the helpers will first require
U != 0, U < PHYS_BASE, and size <= PHYS_BASE - U. Integer subtraction
avoids computing a wrapped end address. They will then process one page
fragment at a time.

A planned pagedir_user_page() helper in pagedir.c will use lookup_page()
once to check a present user PTE and return its mapped kernel alias. For
a destination buffer it will also require PTE_W in that same inspection.
The copy helper will memcpy through the alias, then advance to the next
page. A writable kernel alias alone is not proof that the user mapping
permits writes. Bounded string copying will likewise stop at a terminator
or its length limit, checking each new user page before reading it.

read() and write() will validate the complete user span before I/O. A
page-sized heap bounce buffer will carry data between checked copies and
file operations. The filesystem lock will cover only operations on kernel
buffers; user copying and keyboard waits will occur outside that lock.
Small console writes will be copied completely and passed to putbuf() in
one call, including when the user source crosses a page boundary.
Descriptor zero will read keyboard bytes with input_getc() outside the
filesystem lock. Descriptor one will use the console path.

A valid zero-byte read or write will return zero without dereferencing
the buffer; invalid descriptors will still be rejected. Project 2 has one
thread per process and no eviction or unmapping, so the process's mappings
cannot disappear between validation and copying during a syscall.

>> B4: Suppose a system call causes a full page (4,096 bytes) of data
>> to be copied from user space into the kernel.  What is the least
>> and the greatest possible number of inspections of the page table
>> (e.g. calls to pagedir_get_page()) that might result?  What about
>> for a system call that only copies 2 bytes of data?  Is there room
>> for improvement in these numbers, and how much?

Here one inspection means one call to the planned combined translation
and permission helper. These counts cover the data buffer only, excluding
the separate reads of the syscall number and arguments.

A single page-by-page copy of 4,096 bytes touches one page when aligned
and two when unaligned: one to two inspections. Two bytes likewise touch
one page normally or two across a boundary: one to two inspections.

The proposed read/write path first validates the entire buffer, then
performs a checked copy. Those two passes therefore require two to four
inspections for either size. The combined helper checks PTE_W without
another page-table lookup. Zero bytes require no buffer inspection.

Keeping validated translations for the copy could reduce two passes to
one, giving one to two inspections. The initial design will instead
repeat the bounded page checks, avoiding a potentially large allocation
of cached translations. A naive single copy pass that checks each byte
would cost 4,096 or two lookups respectively. Checking once per touched
page is especially useful for large buffers.

>> B5: Briefly describe your implementation of the "wait" system call
>> and how it interacts with process termination.

process_wait(pid) will search only the caller's children list. A missing
record will return -1 immediately. On a match, it will remove the record
before waiting; this claims the only permitted wait for that child.

The caller will keep its ownership reference locally while it blocks on
exit_done. After waking, it will read exit_status, drop that reference,
and return the result. The user wait syscall will call process_wait().
The initial kernel thread will use the same mechanism to keep Pintos
alive until the initial user process finishes.

The child will free its process resources before publishing its status
and signaling exit_done. It will then release its own reference. If the
child exited earlier, the semaphore's stored signal will make wait return
without blocking. Only the small shared record survives until a parent
waits or exits; the child's thread and address space are not retained.

exit(status) will store the supplied result. User exceptions will retain
the default -1. The exit path will print the full executable name without
arguments once, as "%s: exit(%d)\n", before freeing that name. Kernel
threads and halt() will not print a user-process termination message.

>> B6: Any access to user program memory at a user-specified address
>> can fail due to a bad pointer value.  Such accesses must cause the
>> process to be terminated.  System calls are fraught with such
>> accesses, e.g. a "write" system call requires reading the system
>> call number from the user stack, then each of the call's three
>> arguments, then an arbitrary amount of user memory, and any of
>> these can fail at any point.  This poses a design and
>> error-handling problem: how do you best avoid obscuring the primary
>> function of code in a morass of error-handling?  Furthermore, when
>> an error is detected, how do you ensure that all temporarily
>> allocated resources (locks, buffers, etc.) are freed?  In a few
>> paragraphs, describe the strategy or strategies you adopted for
>> managing these issues.  Give an example.

User-memory helpers will return a success flag instead of terminating
the process themselves. Each syscall will first copy its arguments and
any filename into kernel-owned storage. Buffer checks will happen before
the filesystem lock is acquired. This keeps unchecked user accesses out
of filesystem critical sections.

Each operation will have one cleanup path for its temporary resources.
It will release any held lock, free bounce or string buffers, and close
any handle that has not been attached to an FD entry. Only then will it
return an error or terminate for an invalid user address. The common exit
path will close installed FDs and the executable, release child-record
references, free the process name, and destroy the page directory.

For example, read() will check its descriptor and destination span, then
allocate a bounce page. It will lock the filesystem, read into that page,
and unlock before copying to the user buffer. If copying fails, it will
free the bounce page and exit(-1). No filesystem lock remains held and no
temporary page is lost. Allocation failures and short reads will use the
same cleanup path.

Unsigned file sizes and seek positions above the supported signed off_t
range will be rejected before conversion. Bad descriptors will return
an error or make close() a no-op. These cases will not reach filesystem
assertions or leave partially installed descriptor records.

---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> B7: The "exec" system call returns -1 if loading the new executable
>> fails, so it cannot return before the new executable has completed
>> loading.  How does your code ensure this?  How is the load
>> success/failure status passed back to the thread that calls "exec"?

Before thread_create(), process_execute() will copy the command line,
allocate a child_status and startup record, initialize both semaphores
to zero, and give the parent and future child one reference each. The
record will be linked into the parent's child list before creation.
Creation failure will unlink and directly free the status record along
with the command and startup allocations; no child owns that reference.

The child will set the record's pid from thread_tid() before reporting
loading. It will open and deny writes to the executable under the shared
filesystem lock, load the ELF segments, and build the complete argument
stack. Only after all of these succeed will it store load_ok = true and
signal load_done. The parent will wait on that semaphore before deciding
whether to return the pid.

On failure, the child will clean up its partially loaded process before
reporting load_ok = false. Both success and failure will set load_reported
before signaling load_done. The parent will remove that failed child's
record, release its reference, and return -1. Failed executions will not
be waitable children. A child that has not yet reported loading will do
so from its exit path, ensuring that the parent is always awakened.

Neither the filesystem lock nor the record's reference lock will be held
while the parent waits. Semaphore signaling follows the result write and
keeps the record alive until the signaling operation has returned.

>> B8: Consider parent process P with child process C.  How do you
>> ensure proper synchronization and avoid race conditions when P
>> calls wait(C) before C exits?  After C exits?  How do you ensure
>> that all resources are freed in each case?  How about when P
>> terminates without waiting, before C exits?  After C exits?  Are
>> there any special cases?

The shared record will start with two references: one owned by the
parent and one by the child. ref_lock will protect reference-count
changes; the last owner will release the lock before freeing the record.
Neither owner will access the record after dropping its reference.

    P waits first:  P keeps its reference and sleeps on exit_done.
                    C cleans up, stores its status, and signals P.
    C exits first: C cleans up and signals exit_done without waiting.
                    P later consumes the stored signal and status.
    P exits first: P detaches the record and drops its reference.
                    C later cleans up and frees the last reference.
    C then P exit: C leaves only the parent's small record reference.
                    P releases it during its own cleanup.

Children will not keep pointers to a parent's struct thread and will not
be adopted by grandparents. Removing a record when wait begins prevents
a second wait. Failed loads and failed thread creation will use the
special cleanup paths described in B7.

The child will publish exit_status and signal exit_done only after closing
files, releasing its own child references, and destroying its user address
space. It will preserve the existing destruction order: clear pagedir,
activate the kernel page directory, then destroy the old directory.
The child's reference will remain held through its final semaphore calls.

Reference locks will cover only short ownership updates. They will not
be held during filesystem operations, either completion wait, or free().
The filesystem lock will never be held across an exec or wait semaphore.
This permits children to load or exit while a parent is blocked.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> B9: Why did you choose to implement access to user memory from the
>> kernel in the way that you did?

Checking user mappings before copying gives the code an ordinary
failure path. It avoids relying on a kernel page-fault recovery protocol
to discover a bad syscall argument while a lock or allocation is live.
Combining translation and permission checks also prevents writes through
a kernel alias from bypassing a read-only user mapping.

The cost is explicit page checks and a second pass for read/write buffer
validation. That is acceptable for this project, where mappings remain
stable. A later virtual-memory implementation would need to add frame
pinning or an equivalent mechanism before performing blocking I/O.

>> B10: What advantages or disadvantages can you see to your design
>> for file descriptors?

A list allocates descriptor storage only for open files and has no
small fixed table limit. Each entry is easy to remove and close during
normal close() or process cleanup. Separate file handles provide
the independent offsets required by the interface.

Lookup is linear in the number of descriptors. A table would make lookup
faster, but would need a chosen capacity or resizing logic. Monotonic
numbers also eventually exhaust the int range; the wide counter will
detect that condition and make open() fail rather than wrap. Closed
numbers will not be reused in this initial design.

>> B11: The default tid_t to pid_t mapping is the identity mapping.
>> If you changed it, what advantages are there to your approach?

The design will retain the identity mapping: the pid is the tid of
the user process's thread. Each process has exactly one thread, so a
separate PID allocator would add state without helping this assignment.
The parent-child record, not the number alone, determines whether a
process is eligible for wait().
